$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: template-website/winning-trimming-com-au/system-api-key
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-16"
summary: >-
  Enables Payload API key authentication on the Users collection so internal automation (the
  Messenger-to-quote marketing agent) can submit quote requests via POST /api/quotes without being
  throttled by the anonymous IP rate limiter (3/hour). Authenticated API-key requests bypass the
  rate limiter entirely; anonymous web submissions remain rate-limited. The API key is passed via
  the Authorization header as 'users API-Key <key>'.
$system: template-website/system
$service: template-website/winning-trimming-com-au
intent: >-
  The marketing agent lodges Messenger-sourced quote requests (source: 'messenger-ad') through the
  same POST /api/quotes endpoint as the public web form. Because it authenticates via session cookie
  (POST /api/users/login), each request shares the VPS's egress IP, so the agent hits the 3/hour
  rate limit after three quotes. An API key on the Users collection gives the agent a stable,
  header-based credential that Payload recognises as an authenticated user, allowing the
  beforeChange hook to skip the rate limiter for authenticated staff requests while keeping it
  enforced for anonymous public submissions.
status: built
implementation:
  primary: >-
    src/collections/Users/index.ts, src/collections/Quotes.ts,
    src/migrations/20260917_000000_users_api_key.ts
  test_code: ""
  test_code_status: none
see_also:
  - template-website/winning-trimming-com-au/quote-pipeline
  - template-website/winning-trimming-com-au/access-control
flows:
  - id: agent-quote-submission
    name: Marketing agent submits a Messenger quote via API key
    description: >-
      The Messenger-to-quote script authenticates with an API key instead of a session cookie,
      bypassing the IP rate limiter.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: generate
        target: >-
          Admin generates an API key for a staff user via the Payload admin panel (Users collection,
          API Key field)
      - id: s2
        action: configure
        target: >-
          API key stored in the agent's environment (e.g. WT_API_KEY in
          ~/.config/winning-trimming/admin.env)
      - id: s3
        action: submit
        target: >-
          Agent sends POST /api/quotes with header Authorization: 'users API-Key <key>' and the
          quote payload (source: 'messenger-ad', contactPhone required, contactEmail optional)
      - id: s4
        action: authenticate
        target: >-
          Payload's apiKey auth strategy reads the header, hashes the key (SHA-256 HMAC with payload
          secret), finds the matching user, sets req.user with collection='users',
          _strategy='api-key'
      - id: s5
        action: validate
        target: "beforeChange: honeypot check still runs (agent does not fill the website field)"
      - id: s6
        action: skip
        target: "beforeChange: rate limiter is skipped because req.user is set (authenticated request)"
      - id: s7
        action: create
        target: >-
          beforeChange: auto-customer creation runs if contactEmail is present; skipped for
          messenger-ad leads with no email
      - id: s8
        action: send
        target: >-
          afterChange: staff notification email fires; customer email skipped if no contactEmail;
          Meta CAPI Lead event fires
contracts:
  - id: users-api-key-enabled
    description: >-
      The Users collection has useAPIKey: true so Payload generates and validates API keys for staff
      users
    must_have:
      - Users collection auth.useAPIKey = true
      - API key field appears in the admin panel for each user
      - "API key is passed via Authorization: 'users API-Key <key>' header"
      - Payload's built-in apiKey auth strategy handles authentication — no custom code needed
  - id: rate-limit-bypass-for-authenticated
    description: >-
      The beforeChange rate limiter skips authenticated requests (req.user is set) and only applies
      to anonymous submissions
    must_have:
      - If req.user exists (any collection, any strategy), skip checkRateLimit entirely
      - Anonymous requests (no req.user) still hit the 3/hour/IP limit
      - The rate limit check moves from 'ip !== unknown' to 'no req.user AND ip !== unknown'
      - Honeypot check still runs for ALL requests (authenticated or not)
  - id: no-access-rule-changes
    description: >-
      No changes to collection access rules — API-key auth sets req.user the same way session auth
      does, so existing access functions work unchanged
    must_have:
      - Quotes create access stays () => true
      - Quotes read access unchanged (staff see all, customers see own)
      - Users collection access unchanged (authenticated only)
      - No new access functions or middleware
  - id: agent-credential-migration
    description: >-
      The marketing agent script (wt_chat_to_quote.py) switches from session-cookie auth to API-key
      header auth. Owned by the marketing agent team — documented here for completeness.
    must_have:
      - Agent reads WT_API_KEY from environment (admin.env)
      - >-
        Agent sends Authorization: 'users API-Key <key>' header instead of POST /api/users/login +
        cookie jar
      - >-
        Existing session-based auth still works (not removed) — API key is an alternative, not a
        replacement
      - If WT_API_KEY is not set, agent falls back to session login (backward compatible)
tests:
  - id: anonymous-still-rate-limited
    setup:
      method: POST /api/quotes
      auth: none
      ip: 203.0.113.1
      submissions: 4
    expect:
      - count: 3
        result: ok
      - count: 1
        result: rate-limited
  - id: api-key-bypasses-rate-limit
    setup:
      method: POST /api/quotes
      auth: users API-Key <valid-key>
      ip: 203.0.113.1
      submissions: 10
    expect:
      - count: 10
        result: ok
  - id: invalid-api-key-rejected
    setup:
      method: POST /api/quotes
      auth: users API-Key invalid-key
      ip: 203.0.113.1
    expect:
      - result: created-as-anonymous
      - note: >-
          invalid key means req.user is null, so it falls through to anonymous path with rate
          limiting
  - id: api-key-auth-sets-req-user
    setup:
      method: GET /api/users/me
      auth: users API-Key <valid-key>
    expect:
      - field: user.collection
        result: users
      - field: user._strategy
        result: api-key
  - id: honeypot-still-blocks-authenticated
    setup:
      method: POST /api/quotes
      auth: users API-Key <valid-key>
      website: bot-spam
    expect:
      - result: error response
decisions:
  - id: payload-built-in-api-key
    decision: >-
      Use Payload's built-in useAPIKey on the Users collection rather than a custom middleware or a
      separate API-key collection
    rationale: >-
      Payload 3.x has first-class API key support: set auth.useAPIKey=true on the collection, and
      the built-in apiKey auth strategy handles header parsing, HMAC hashing, and user lookup. No
      custom code needed for authentication — only a one-line bypass in the rate limiter. The API
      key is stored as an HMAC hash (apiKeyIndex) in the DB, so a DB leak doesn't expose the raw
      key.
    date: "2026-09-17"
  - id: bypass-not-higher-limit
    decision: Authenticated requests bypass the rate limiter entirely rather than getting a higher limit
    rationale: >-
      The rate limiter exists to prevent anonymous spam/abuse. Authenticated staff requests via API
      key are trusted by definition — a higher limit (e.g. 100/hour) would just be an arbitrary
      number that could still be hit during a busy Messenger campaign. Bypassing entirely is simpler
      and correct: the honeypot still guards against automated abuse even for authenticated
      requests.
    date: "2026-09-17"
  - id: check-req-user-not-strategy
    decision: >-
      The rate limiter bypass checks req.user (any authenticated user) rather than checking
      _strategy === 'api-key' specifically
    rationale: >-
      The rate limiter should not run for ANY authenticated request — whether via API key, session
      cookie, or a future auth strategy. Checking req.user is simpler, more future-proof, and
      matches the intent (rate limiting is for anonymous public submissions only).
      Session-authenticated customers submitting quotes are also exempt, which is correct — they are
      known users, not anonymous spammers.
    date: "2026-09-17"
